  "answer_core": "I would not choose a dissertation direction yet. Treat the next two weeks as a bounded, low‑cost test rather than a commitment. The real decision turns on two gates.\n\nFirst, the Silva/data gate: confirm that Dr. Silva has usable data you can access on a clear timeline. Identify a collaborator who can compensate for your current lab’s single‑cell gaps. Ask what data exists, who controls it, when you can inspect it, what authorship or collaboration terms would apply, and what work would still be yours. Do not assume advisor support; get explicit backing for this direction.\n\nSecond, the fallback gate: verify that your current path can still carry a dissertation if the Silva probe fails. It only remains a fallback if your advisor keeps it resourced, available, and technically ready for you to re‑enter, and if you set a clear stop‑loss date with visible conditions for walking away.\n\nKeep the political sequence clean. Talk to your advisor first. Use Dr. Silva only as a low‑cost reality check on concrete data access, collaboration depth, and timing. Go to the committee only after you know whether the data path is real and whether your advisor would support the move without quietly pulling you back toward the safer lab path.\n\nFor the next two weeks, run dated evidence checks instead of building a finished proposal. Do not treat broad PhD success‑rate claims as calibrated probabilities for this case; use them only as humility checks. Do not run all three options in parallel. Choose the path only after both gates have concrete evidence. The best path is not the hottest story; it is the one with a real chance of becoming a finishable PhD.",
